This implementation uses a single buffer to store all log messages, and flushes the buffer to the cloud logging service every time it is full or when it is explicitly requested. The `log` function takes a variable number of arguments, formats the message using `snprintf`, and appends it to the buffer. The `flush` function sends the buffered log messages to the cloud logging service using curl.

The `main` function initializes the logging system by allocating memory for the log buffer and creating threads to handle log writes. The `log` and `flush` functions are thread-safe, as they use a mutex to synchronize access to the shared log buffer. The `cleanup` function releases all allocated resources when the application exits.

To optimize performance, this implementation uses a single global buffer for all log messages, which reduces memory allocation overhead and improves performance by reducing cache misses. It also uses a fixed thread pool of 16 threads to handle log writes concurrently, allowing it to handle high-throughput logging without significant degradation in response time or increased latency.

This implementation also includes error handling and circuit breaker patterns to protect the logging system from cascading failures. If an API rate limit is exceeded or a network failure occurs during log write, the utility will retry the failed request with exponential backoff and take appropriate actions to prevent further issues.']

