Here is a proposed design for the image processing library:

1. Image Format Support: The library should support multiple image formats such as JPEG, PNG, GIF, and others if necessary. It should be able to read and write these formats efficiently and handle various edge cases such as corrupted or incomplete files.
2. Metadata Extraction: The library should be able to extract detailed metadata from images such as orientation, compression type, resolution, and embedded profiles like ICC. It should do this efficiently and accurately, handling different types of image files and potential issues with the metadata.
3. Error Handling: The library should have robust error handling mechanisms in place to manage unsupported orientations, incomplete files, or corrupted metadata. This includes gracefully handling errors that may occur during processing, such as file not found or invalid file format.
4. Concurrency: The library should be designed to minimize processing time without compromising accuracy, especially for large images or batches of images. It should use concurrency where appropriate to handle multiple images at the same time.
5. Memory Management: Developers should decide how to manage memory efficiently when dealing with multiple embedded profiles or large images. The library should balance between memory usage and speed, ensuring that it can handle high volumes of image processing tasks concurrently.
6. API: The library should expose a user-friendly API that abstracts complex operations, allowing users to easily query metadata without deep understanding of underlying processes. The API should be well-documented, making it easy for developers to use and understand the library's functionality.
7. Maintainability: The library should prioritize maintainability through clear code structure and comprehensive documentation. This includes providing clear documentation for each function and method, as well as regularly updating the documentation to reflect changes in the library over time.
8. Edge Cases and Integration: During development, developers should consider edge cases and potential integration issues with other systems. They should ensure that the library can handle these cases gracefully and integrate with other libraries or systems seamlessly.

Here is an example of how this could be implemented in code:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <jpeglib.h>
#include <pnglib.h>
#include <giflib.h>
#include <icc_profile.h>

// Define the metadata structure to hold all the information extracted from an image
struct Metadata {
    int orientation;
    char* compression_type;
    int resolution[2];
    struct icc_profile* embedded_profile;
};

// Define the function to extract metadata from an image
void extract_metadata(const unsigned char* data, size_t length, struct Metadata* metadata) {
    // Check if the data is a valid JPEG file and extract the orientation if so
    struct jpeg_decompress_struct cinfo;
    cinfo.err = jpeg_std_error(&cinfo);
    jpeg_create_decompress(&cinfo);
    if (jpeg_read_header(&cinfo, TRUE) == JPEG_SUSPENDED) {
        metadata->orientation = cinfo.image_info.orientation;
    } else {
        metadata->orientation = 0;
    }
    jpeg_destroy_decompress(&cinfo);

    // Check if the data is a valid PNG file and extract the resolution if so
    struct png_struct* png = png_create_read_struct(PNG_LIBPNG_VER_STRING, NULL, NULL, NULL);
    if (png != NULL) {
        metadata->resolution[0] = png_get_image_width(png);
        metadata->resolution[1] = png_get_image_height(png);
    } else {
        metadata->resolution[0] = 0;
        metadata->resolution[1] = 0;
    }

    // Check if the data is a valid GIF file and extract the embedded profile if so
    struct gif_lib* gif = gif_create_read(data, length);
    if (gif != NULL) {
        metadata->embedded_profile = gif_get_icc_profile(gif);
    } else {
        metadata->embedded_profile = NULL;
    }

    // Free any memory that was allocated during extraction
    png_destroy_read_struct(&png, &info, &end);
    gif_free(&gif);
}

// Define the function to handle errors during metadata extraction
void handle_error(const char* message) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Error: %s\n", message);
}

int main() {
    // Load an image from disk and extract its metadata
    const unsigned char* data = load_image("example.jpg");
    size_t length = strlen(data);
    struct Metadata metadata;
    extract_metadata(data, length, &metadata);

    // Print the extracted metadata
    printf("Metadata:\n");
    printf("Orientation: %d\n", metadata.orientation);
    printf("Compression type: %s\n", metadata.compression_type);
    printf("Resolution: %dx%d\n", metadata.resolution[0], metadata.resolution[1]);
    if (metadata.embedded_profile != NULL) {
        printf("ICC profile:\n");
        for (int i = 0; i < metadata.embedded_profile->length; i++) {
            printf("%d ", metadata.embedded_profile->profile[i]);
        }
    }

    // Free any memory that was allocated during extraction
    free(data);
}
```
