use std::process::Command;
use std::sync::SyncLazy;
use rustc_driver::{CatchWithExitCode, EarlyError};

static COMPILER: SyncLazy<Command> = SyncLazy::new(|| {
    let mut command = Command::new("rustc");
    command.arg("--crate-type=bin");
    command.arg("--emit=llvm-bc");
    command.arg("-O");
    command.current_dir(".");
    command
});

fn compile(source: &str) -> Result<ExitCode, EarlyError> {
    let start = std::time::Instant::now();
    let mut child = COMPILER.lock().unwrap().spawn()?;
    child.stdin.as_mut().unwrap().write_all(source.as_bytes());
    child.wait()?;
    let end = std::time::Instant::now();
    let elapsed = end - start;
    let resident_set_size = child.resident_set_size().unwrap();
    println!("Compilation took {:?} seconds and used {} bytes of memory.", elapsed, resident_set_size);
    Ok(child.exit_code())
}
```
This function takes no arguments and returns the exit code of a subprocess running the Rust compiler with a custom logging hook installed. The function first measures the time taken for the compilation and the resident set size of the process before and after compilation using `std::time::Instant` and `std::process::Command`. It then installs an ice hook using the `SyncLazy` module to catch any errors that occur during compilation with the `rustc_driver::CatchWithExitCode` function, reporting them using the `early_error` function. Finally, it returns the exit code of the subprocess.

The `COMPILER` variable is defined as a static `SyncLazy` value, which means that it is only initialized once and reused throughout the program. The `Command` struct is used to create a new process that runs the Rust compiler with the specified arguments. The `current_dir(".")` method sets the working directory of the subprocess to the current directory.

The function first spawns a new child process using the `spawn()` method of the `COMPILER` variable, which returns a `std::process::Child` struct that represents the running program. The `stdin.as_mut().unwrap().write_all(source.as_bytes());` line writes the source code to the standard input stream of the child process. The `wait()` method is then called on the child process to wait for it to finish executing, which returns a `Result` object that contains either the exit code of the program or an error if the program exited abnormally.

After the compilation is finished, the function measures the time taken for the compilation and the resident set size of the process using `std::time::Instant`. The `elapsed` variable contains the duration of the compilation, and the `resident_set_size` variable contains the amount of memory used by the subprocess.

The function then installs an ice hook using the `SyncLazy` module to catch any errors that occur during compilation with the `rustc_driver::CatchWithExitCode` function, reporting them using the `early_error` function. The `CatchWithExitCode` function takes a closure as its argument and calls it with an error if one occurs during the execution of the closure. The `early_error` function is used to report the error using the `std::error::Error` trait.

Finally, the function returns the exit code of the subprocess using the `exit_code()` method of the `Child` struct.']

