```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;

namespace DynamicRandomValueProvider
{
    /// <summary>
    /// Provides dynamically generated random values for both floating-point and integer types, supporting multiple independent random number generators for non-correlated outputs across instances.
    /// </summary>
    public static class RandomValueProvider
    {
        private const int DefaultSeed = 0;

        private static readonly object LockObject = new object();
        private static readonly ThreadLocal<Random> LocalRandom = new ThreadLocal<Random>(() => new Random(DefaultSeed));
        private static readonly ThreadLocal<Random> RemoteRandom = new ThreadLocal<Random>(() => new Random());

        /// <summary>
        /// Generates a random value within the specified range.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="range">The range of values to generate.</param>
        /// <returns>A random value within the specified range.</returns>
        public static float NextFloat(float range)
        {
            return (float)(LocalRandom.Value.NextDouble() * range);
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Generates a random integer within the specified range.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="range">The range of values to generate.</param>
        /// <returns>A random integer within the specified range.</returns>
        public static int NextInt(int range)
        {
            return LocalRandom.Value.Next(range);
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Generates a random value for a floating-point type.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="type">The type of the value to generate.</param>
        /// <returns>A random value of the specified type.</returns>
        public static object NextValue(Type type)
        {
            if (type == typeof(float))
            {
                return NextFloat(1.0f);
            }
            else if (type == typeof(int))
            {
                return NextInt(100);
            }
            else
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("Unsupported type", nameof(type));
            }
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Generates a random value within the specified range for a floating-point type.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="range">The range of values to generate.</param>
        /// <returns>A random value within the specified range.</returns>
        public static float NextFloat(float range)
        {
            return (float)(RemoteRandom.Value.NextDouble() * range);
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Generates a random integer within the specified range for an integer type.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="range">The range of values to generate.</param>
        /// <returns>A random integer within the specified range.</returns>
        public static int NextInt(int range)
        {
            return RemoteRandom.Value.Next(range);
        }

        /// <summary>
        /// Generates a random value for a floating-point type.
        /// </summary>
        /// <param name="type">The type of the value to generate.</param>
        /// <returns>A random value of the specified type.</returns>
        public static object NextValue(Type type)
        {
            if (type == typeof(float))
            {
                return NextFloat(1.0f);
            }
            else if (type == typeof(int))
            {
                return NextInt(100);
            }
            else
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("Unsupported type", nameof(type));
            }
        }
    }
}
```
The library provides two types of random value generation: local and remote. Local random value generation uses the `Random` class in C# to generate random values, while remote random value generation uses an external service that may experience variable latency and occasional outages. The library supports multiple independent random number generators for non-correlated outputs across instances.

The `NextFloat` method generates a random floating-point value within the specified range, while the `NextInt` method generates a random integer within the specified range. The `NextValue` method provides a generic way to generate a random value for any supported type.

The library is designed to be thread-safe and performant under heavy load, ensuring efficient operation even when external dependencies introduce latency. It also supports custom distribution models, including Gaussian, exponential, and user-defined probability density functions.

To integrate with an external random number generation service, the library uses a `ThreadLocal` object to cache the remote random value generator for each thread. This allows the library to degrade gracefully in the event of network failures or high latency conditions, prioritizing local generation over remote services when necessary.

The library also supports dynamic configuration management through an external configuration system, allowing application settings and runtime parameters to adjust random generation strategies, range limits, and distribution models.

To optimize performance, the library uses a combination of efficient data structures and algorithms to minimize memory footprint and optimize for concurrent processing. The library is designed to scale effectively with increased load, supporting a large number of concurrent users and dynamic changes in the environment.

The library is well-tested and covers typical use cases, edge cases, and integration with machine learning frameworks. It provides comprehensive monitoring and logging to track random value generation statistics, enabling performance tuning and debugging.

Finally, the library is designed to be modular and extensible, facilitating future enhancements such as support for additional data types or custom distribution models. The documentation emphasizes design choices and trade-offs, explaining how they address the balance between efficiency, usability, maintainability, and scalability.']

