The `start()` method starts the service and logs metrics using the `log()` method. The method waits until the service is ready before returning. The `stop()` method stops the service and logs metrics using the same `log()` method. The method also waits until the service is shutdown before returning.

The `log()` method uses a custom C function to format and append logs to a centralized logging system. The function uses `snprintf` and `va_start/va_end` to include timestamps, service ID, and operation duration in the log message. The function also handles potential edge cases such as network failures when sending logs to remote servers and unexpected delays in service start-stop processes by providing mechanisms for retrying failed operations and maintaining log integrity under heavy load.

The `Logger` class is used to handle logging to different backends (e.g., local file, remote server, cloud storage). The class provides a single method for logging messages that can be used by the `ServiceMonitor` class. The `Logger` class also includes mechanisms for retrying failed operations and maintaining log integrity under heavy load.

