fn create_directory(name: &str, env_var: &str) -> Result<(), ()> {
    if let Some(path) = std::env::get(env_var).and_then(|v| v.to_string()) {
        // Use the existing value of the environment variable
        return Ok(());
    }

    // Create a new directory with the specified name
    let dir = fs::create_dir(name)?;

    // Set the environment variable to the path of the new directory
    std::env::set(env_var, dir.to_str().unwrap())?;

    Ok(())
}
```
This function first checks if the environment variable is already set by using the `std::env::get` method and checking if it returns `Some`. If it does, the function uses the existing value of the environment variable and returns. Otherwise, it creates a new directory with the specified name using the `fs::create_dir` method and sets the environment variable to the path of the new directory using the `std::env::set` method.

The `Result<(), ()>` return type is used to indicate whether the function was successful in creating the directory and setting the environment variable. If the function was not successful, it returns an error value of `()` instead.

