void insert_string(const std::forward_list<std::string>& list, const std::string& to_insert, const std::string& insert_after) {
    // Find the position of the first occurrence of `insert_after` in the list
    auto it = std::find(list.begin(), list.end(), insert_after);

    // If `insert_after` was not found in the list, insert the string at the end
    if (it == list.end()) {
        list.push_back(to_insert);
    } else {
        // Insert the string after the first occurrence of `insert_after`
        list.insert_after(it, to_insert);
    }
}
```
This function uses the `std::find` algorithm to find the position of the first occurrence of the `insert_after` string in the forward list. If the `insert_after` string was not found in the list, the function inserts the `to_insert` string at the end of the list using the `push_back` method. Otherwise, it uses the `insert_after` method to insert the `to_insert` string after the first occurrence of `insert_after`.

The function does not return anything and modifies the forward list in place by inserting or appending the `to_insert` string as appropriate.']

