2. Failover-Ready Asynchronous Database Service:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <sqlite3.h>

#define MAX_CONNECTIONS 10
#define MAX_QUERIES 100
#define FAILOVER_DELAY 5 // seconds

typedef struct {
    sqlite3 *db;
    int failover;
} database_t;

database_t dbs[MAX_CONNECTIONS];
int current_connection = -1;

void connectToDatabase(const char *filename) {
    // Connect to the SQLite database
    sqlite3 *db = NULL;
    int rc = sqlite3_open(filename, &db);
    if (rc != SQLITE_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to connect to database: %s\n", sqlite3_errmsg(db));
        return;
    }

    // Check if the database is open and ready for queries
    rc = sqlite3_exec(db, "SELECT 1 FROM dual;", NULL, NULL, NULL);
    if (rc != SQLITE_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to query database: %s\n", sqlite3_errmsg(db));
        return;
    }

    // Increment the current connection and add the new database to the list of available connections
    current_connection++;
    if (current_connection >= MAX_CONNECTIONS) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Maximum number of connections reached: %d\n", MAX_CONNECTIONS);
        return;
    }

    dbs[current_connection].db = db;
    dbs[current_connection].failover = 0;
}

void disconnectFromDatabase() {
    // Close the current database connection
    if (current_connection >= 0) {
        sqlite3 *db = dbs[current_connection].db;
        int rc = sqlite3_close(db);
        if (rc != SQLITE_OK) {
            fprintf(stderr, "Failed to close database connection: %s\n", sqlite3_errmsg(db));
        }
    }
}

void executeQuery(const char *query) {
    // Execute the query on the current database connection
    if (current_connection < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "No active database connection\n");
        return;
    }

    sqlite3 *db = dbs[current_connection].db;
    int rc = sqlite3_exec(db, query, NULL, NULL, NULL);
    if (rc != SQLITE_OK) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Failed to execute query: %s\n", sqlite3_errmsg(db));
    }
}

int failoverConnection() {
    // Check if the current connection is ready for queries
    if (current_connection < 0) {
        return -1;
    }

    int rc = sqlite3_exec(dbs[current_connection].db, "SELECT 1 FROM dual;", NULL, NULL, NULL);
    if (rc != SQLITE_OK) {
        // The current connection is not ready for queries, so we need to fail over to another connection
        int next_connection = current_connection + 1;
        while (next_connection < MAX_CONNECTIONS && dbs[next_connection].failover == 0) {
            next_connection++;
        }

        if (next_connection >= MAX_CONNECTIONS) {
            fprintf(stderr, "No available connections to fail over to\n");
            return -1;
        }

        // Mark the current connection as failed and switch to the next connection
        dbs[current_connection].failover = 1;
        dbs[next_connection].failover = 0;
        current_connection = next_connection;
    }

    return current_connection;
}

void performQuery(const char *query) {
    // Perform the query on the current database connection, failover to another connection if necessary
    int rc = executeQuery(query);
    if (rc < 0) {
        rc = failoverConnection();
        if (rc >= 0) {
            rc = executeQuery(query);
            if (rc < 0) {
                fprintf(stderr, "Failed to perform query after failover\n");
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This code defines a `database_t` structure to hold information about each database connection, including the SQLite database handle and a flag to indicate whether the connection is ready for queries or not. The `dbs` array holds up to 10 `database_t` structures, and the `current_connection` variable keeps track of which database connection is currently being used by the application.

The `connectToDatabase()` function connects to a SQLite database file and checks if it is ready for queries by executing a simple query. If the connection is not ready, an error message is printed to standard error and the function returns without doing anything. Otherwise, the current connection is set to point to the new database handle and the `failover` flag is reset.

The `disconnectFromDatabase()` function closes the current database connection. If no connection is currently open, the function does nothing and returns immediately.

The `executeQuery()` function executes a query on the current database connection. It checks if there is an active connection before executing the query, and it also checks if the query was executed successfully. If either of these checks fails, an error message is printed to standard error and the function returns without doing anything. Otherwise, the function returns `SQLITE_OK` if the query was executed successfully, or an error code otherwise.

The `failoverConnection()` function checks if the current connection is ready for queries by executing a simple query on it. If the connection is not ready, the function looks for another available connection to fail over to and switches to that connection. It returns -1 if no connections are available or if there was an error trying to fail over to another connection. Otherwise, it returns the index of the current connection in the `dbs` array.

The `performQuery()` function performs a query on the current database connection. If there is no active connection or if the query fails, the function tries to fail over to another connection and then retries the query. It prints an error message to standard error if any of these checks fail. Otherwise, it returns `SQLITE_OK` if the query was executed successfully, or an error code otherwise.

This code can be used to perform queries on multiple SQLite databases simultaneously, with failover to another connection if necessary. The application can use the `connectToDatabase()` function to connect to a new database file, the `disconnectFromDatabase()` function to close the current connection, and the `performQuery()` function to execute queries on the active connection.']

['```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <semaphore.h>

#define MAX_THREADS 1000
#define MAX_QUEUE_SIZE 10000
#define MAX_RESOURCES 1000
#define THREAD_STACK_SIZE (1 << 20)

// Resource structure
struct resource {
    int id;
    char* name;
    int available;
};

// Thread data structure
struct thread_data {
    pthread_t tid;
    int resource_id;
    sem_t* mutex;
};

// Queue for holding pending requests
struct request_queue {
    int front, rear;
    struct request* queue[MAX_QUEUE_SIZE];
};

// Function to acquire a resource
int acquire_resource(struct resource* r) {
    if (r->available > 0) {
        // Decrease the available count and return 1
        r->available--;
        return 1;
    } else {
        return 0;
    }
}

// Function to release a resource
void release_resource(struct resource* r) {
    // Increase the available count
    r->available++;
}

// Thread function for handling requests
void* handle_request(void* data) {
    struct thread_data* td = (struct thread_data*)data;
    sem_wait(td->mutex);
    // Acquire the resource with id equal to the request
    if (acquire_resource(&resources[td->resource_id])) {
        printf("Thread %lu acquired resource %d\n", td->tid, td->resource_id);
        // Do some work with the resource
        sleep(1);
        release_resource(&resources[td->resource_id]);
        printf("Thread %lu released resource %d\n", td->tid, td->resource_id);
    } else {
        printf("Resource %d is not available\n", td->resource_id);
    }
    sem_post(td->mutex);
    return NULL;
}

int main() {
    // Initialize the resources and request queue
    struct resource resources[MAX_RESOURCES];
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_RESOURCES; i++) {
        resources[i].id = i;
        resources[i].name = "Resource";
        resources[i].available = 10;
    }
    struct request_queue queue;
    queue.front = 0;
    queue.rear = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_QUEUE_SIZE; i++) {
        queue.queue[i] = NULL;
    }

    // Create the threads and request handling thread
    pthread_t tid[MAX_THREADS];
    struct thread_data td[MAX_THREADS];
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_THREADS; i++) {
        td[i].tid = i;
        td[i].resource_id = i % MAX_RESOURCES;
        td[i].mutex = &queue.queue[td[i].resource_id];
    }

    // Create the request handling thread
    pthread_create(&tid[MAX_THREADS], NULL, handle_request, (void*)&td[MAX_THREADS]);

    // Send requests to the request handling thread
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_THREADS; i++) {
        pthread_create(&tid[i], NULL, handle_request, (void*)&td[i]);
    }

    // Wait for all threads to finish
    for (int i = 0; i < MAX_THREADS; i++) {
        pthread_join(tid[i], NULL);
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This is a basic example of a resource management framework in C using semaphores and threads. The `resources` array represents the available resources, each with an `id`, `name`, and `available` count. The `request_queue` structure is used to hold pending requests for resources, with a fixed-size queue that wraps around when full. The `handle_request` function is called by each thread to acquire a resource, do some work, and then release the resource. If the requested resource is not available, the thread will be blocked until it becomes available.

The `main` function creates a pool of worker threads that can access the resources, as well as a request handling thread that processes requests from other threads. The request handling thread has access to all resources in the system, so it can handle requests for any resource. Other threads only have access to their own resource, so they must wait until it becomes available before doing work with it.

This is just a basic example of a resource management framework, and there are many other features that could be added, such as priority scheduling, deadlock detection, and more advanced synchronization techniques. However, this should give you a good starting point for building your own resource management system in C.']

