3. Once all the dependencies for a script have been resolved, execute the script using `require`.
4. After executing each script, check if any of its dependencies are still unresolved. If there are any, repeat steps 2-3 until all dependencies have been resolved or an error is encountered.
5. If an error is encountered during dependency resolution or script execution, print an error message and stop processing further scripts.
6. Finally, after all scripts have been executed, check if any of the dependencies were not resolved (i.e., there are still missing scripts). If so, print an error message to the user indicating which dependencies are missing.

This function is designed to be called by the `node-scripts` package's `runScripts()` method, which will provide the dependency graph as input and handle any errors that may occur during execution."]

