['Here is an example of how you could design and implement a robust, scalable, and modular C++ solution to integrate with an existing video processing pipeline for asynchronous video transcoding requests:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <future>
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>

// Third-party video processing library
#include "video_processing.h"

// External data sources (metadata repositories and storage services)
#include "external_data_sources.h"

using namespace std;

// Struct to represent a transcoding job
struct TranscodingJob {
    int id;                   // Unique identifier for the job
    string input_file;        // Input file path
    string output_file;       // Output file path
    string format;           // Desired video format (e.g. MP4, AVI, WebM)
    int resolution;          // Desired video resolution (e.g. 720p, 1080p)
    bool urgent;              // Whether the job is considered urgent or not
};

// Struct to represent a transcoding status
struct TranscodingStatus {
    int id;                   // Unique identifier for the job
    string status;            // Current status of the job (e.g. "in progress", "completed")
};

// Caching mechanism for frequently requested video resolutions and formats
unordered_map<string, vector<TranscodingJob>> cache;
mutex cache_mtx;               // Mutex to protect access to the cache
condition_variable cv;         // Condition variable to signal when a job is added or removed from the cache

// Backpressure mechanism to prevent system overload during peak loads
vector<TranscodingJob> pending_jobs;   // List of pending jobs waiting for processing
int max_pending_jobs = 1000;          // Maximum number of pending jobs before backpressure is applied
mutex pending_jobs_mtx;               // Mutex to protect access to the list of pending jobs
condition_variable cv_pending_jobs;   // Condition variable to signal when a job is added or removed from the list of pending jobs
int num_active_threads = 0;          // Number of active threads processing jobs
mutex num_active_threads_mtx;        // Mutex to protect access to the number of active threads
condition_variable cv_num_active_threads;   // Condition variable to signal when a thread becomes inactive or active

// API for submitting transcoding jobs and retrieving status updates
void submitTranscodingJob(const TranscodingJob& job) {
    lock_guard<mutex> lck(cache_mtx);
    if (cache.find(job.input_file) != cache.end()) {
        // If the input file is already in the cache, we can directly return the status
        TranscodingStatus status;
        status.id = job.id;
        status.status = "in progress";
        return status;
    }

    // Otherwise, we add the job to the pending jobs list and signal the condition variable
    {
        lock_guard<mutex> lck(pending_jobs_mtx);
        pending_jobs.push_back(job);
    }
    cv_pending_jobs.notify_one();
}

TranscodingStatus getTranscodingJobStatus(int id) {
    lock_guard<mutex> lck(cache_mtx);
    auto it = cache.find(id);
    if (it != cache.end()) {
        return it->second;
    } else {
        // If the job is not in the cache, we return an empty status
        TranscodingStatus status;
        status.id = id;
        status.status = "not found";
        return status;
    }
}
```
The above code defines a `TranscodingJob` struct to represent a transcoding job and a `TranscodingStatus` struct to represent the status of a transcoding job. The caching mechanism is implemented using an unordered map, where each key corresponds to an input file path and each value is a vector of `TranscodingJob`s that are pending or have been completed for that input file. A mutex and condition variable are used to protect access to the cache and signal when a job is added or removed from the cache.

The backpressure mechanism is implemented using a vector of `TranscodingJob`s, where each job is added to the list as soon as it is submitted and removed as soon as it is processed. A maximum number of pending jobs can be specified before backpressure is applied. A mutex and condition variable are used to protect access to the list of pending jobs and signal when a job becomes active or inactive.

The API for submitting transcoding jobs and retrieving status updates is implemented using two functions: `submitTranscodingJob` and `getTranscodingJobStatus`. The former function takes a `TranscodingJob` object as input and adds the job to the pending jobs list if it is not already in the cache. It signals the condition variable to wake up any threads that may be waiting for new jobs to become available. The latter function takes an integer ID as input and returns the corresponding `TranscodingStatus` object if it is found in the cache, or an empty status if it is not found.

To handle unsupported formats, invalid resolutions, and network interruptions robustly, you can use error handling mechanisms such as exception handling and logging. For example:
```
// Function to perform transcoding on a job
void transcode(const TranscodingJob& job) {
    try {
        // Perform the actual transcoding using the input file and output file specified in the job
        return;
    } catch (exception& e) {
        // If there is an exception, we log it and mark the job as failed
        cout << "Exception caught while transcoding job: " << e.what() << endl;
        job.status = "failed";
    }
}
```
In this example, any exceptions that occur during the actual transcoding process are caught and logged using the `cout` statement. The exception is also used to mark the job as failed in the cache.

To improve performance, you can use multi-threading to process multiple jobs simultaneously. For example:
```
// Function to process pending jobs concurrently
void processPendingJobs() {
    // Lock the mutex to protect access to the list of pending jobs
    lock_guard<mutex> lck(pending_jobs_mtx);

    // While there are still jobs waiting, we continue processing them
    while (!pending_jobs.empty()) {
        TranscodingJob job = pending_jobs.front();
        pending_jobs.pop_front();

        // If the maximum number of active threads has been reached, we wait for a thread to become inactive before continuing
        while (num_active_threads >= max_pending_jobs) {
            cv_num_active_threads.wait(lck);
        }

        // We add the current thread to the list of active threads and signal the condition variable
        num_active_threads++;
        {
            lock_guard<mutex> lck(cv_num_active_threads.mtx());
            cv_num_active_threads.notify_one();
        }

        // We perform the actual transcoding on the job using a separate thread
        thread th([&] {
            transcode(job);
            num_active_threads--;
            {
                lock_guard<mutex> lck(cv_num_active_threads.mtx());
                cv_num_active_threads.notify_one();
            }
        });
        th.detach();
    }
}
```
In this example, a separate thread is created to process each job concurrently using the `transcode` function. The maximum number of active threads can be specified before backpressure is applied. The current thread waits for a thread to become inactive before continuing to process new jobs.']

