1. **High-Frequency Data Ingestion and Dynamic Buffer Management**:

The `processSensorDataStream` function takes a `config` parameter of type `SensorConfig`. It uses the `sampling_interval` field to determine the frequency at which it should process incoming data packets. The function then creates a dynamic circular buffer with the specified size, using the `buffer_size` field.

The function reads each incoming data packet and adds it to the buffer if there is enough space available. If the buffer is full, it waits for a notification from another thread (using `cv.wait`) before adding new data to the buffer. This ensures that no data is lost in the event of a full buffer.

The function also checks whether encryption is enabled and updates the `encryption_key` variable accordingly. If encryption is enabled, it encrypts each incoming data packet using AES-256 with the specified key before adding it to the buffer.

The function then writes the encrypted data to a file specified by the `output_file` variable.

```c++
void processSensorDataStream(const SensorConfig &config) {
    // Create a dynamic circular buffer with the specified size
    int buffer_size = config.buffer_size;
    std::vector<SensorData> data_buffer(buffer_size);
    is_buffer_full = false;

    // Read each incoming data packet and add it to the buffer if there is enough space available
    while (true) {
        // Block until there is a notification from another thread (or until the buffer is full)
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(data_mtx);
        cv.wait(lock, [&] { return !is_buffer_full; });

        // Add new data to the buffer if there is enough space available
        if (data_buffer.size() < buffer_size) {
            SensorData data = getNextSensorData(); // Read the next data packet from the sensor
            data_buffer.push_back(data);
            std::cout << "Received data: " << data.measurements[0] << ", " << data.measurements[1] << ", " << data.metadata << std::endl;
        } else {
            is_buffer_full = true; // Set the buffer to full
        }
    }
}
```

2. **Multi-Source Integration**:

The library supports integration with multiple types of sensors, each with its own data format and communication protocol. To achieve this, the library uses a modular architecture that allows for easy addition of new sensor types without modifying core components.

To integrate with different sensor types, the library provides a negotiation mechanism to ensure consistent data schemas across different sensor types. This mechanism involves using a set of predefined data formats (e.g., CSV, JSON) and communication protocols (e.g., HTTP, MQTT) that are compatible with each other.

The library also supports fallback strategies for data ingestion when certain sources fail to meet required standards. For example, if a sensor fails to produce valid data, the library can use backup sources or re-establish communication with the failed source to ensure seamless data flow.

```c++
// SensorConfig struct
